WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.300
Submilli is the runtime where
agent-written code runs.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.340
Packages give that code typed ways to work
with services,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.500
and blueprints say which operations and
arguments are allowed.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:16.420
The runtime brings those pieces together
and enforces the decision

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.820
before protected work continues.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.860
As the developer, you can write a package
or use one that already exists.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:28.500
A package exposes typed functions for a
service,

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:32.420
and its checkpoint checks the action and
arguments before protected

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.780
work continues.

00:00:35.067 --> 00:00:39.147
You define the rules. Create a blueprint
that allows a billing

00:00:39.227 --> 00:00:43.307
lookup when the requested customer matches
the application’s context.

00:00:43.867 --> 00:00:46.267
Deny other operations by default.

00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:51.853
Your application binds Customer A’s ID as
trusted context

00:00:51.933 --> 00:00:55.933
for the runtime. The package passes the
requested customer into

00:00:56.013 --> 00:01:00.093
its permission check. The runtime compares
that request with the customer

00:01:00.173 --> 00:01:02.333
ID the application selected.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:08.200
The agent writes this program in
Submilli’s curated TypeScript subset.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.560
It reads Customer A’s charges and totals
them.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:16.240
Unsupported API calls and unavailable
imports fail validation

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:20.480
before execution, but valid code still
needs permission.

00:01:21.767 --> 00:01:25.767
The agent calls the package. Before
looking up the charges,

00:01:25.847 --> 00:01:29.367
the package checks the operation and
customer ID.

00:01:29.447 --> 00:01:33.447
The runtime applies the blueprint:
Customer A matches,

00:01:33.527 --> 00:01:35.207
so the lookup proceeds.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.500
Now request Customer B. The code still
passes language

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.820
validation, but the permission check
denies this lookup because Customer

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:48.740
B does not match the application’s
Customer A context.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.080
Submilli runs agent code with the access
you define through packages

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:55.280
and blueprints.
